+Automatic body fitting keeps its calibrated eye reserve through the impact.
+The camera also stays at least 25 cm above the measured upper torso, matching
+standing torso fitting. The neck joint alone is insufficient: the chest mesh
+can rise above that pivot when the landing animation pitches the torso forward.
+Both automatic and manual body scales use the measured surface when available;
+missing or stale outfit measurements retain the existing neck/reserve fallback.
+
+Camera and body reconstruction share this reference, so the higher viewpoint
+does not pull the torso or feet up with it. Clearance follows the current native
+animation, including its dip and recovery; it does not feed a previous rendered
+body pose back into the camera or hold the camera at standing height.
+
+Regression checks compare the final eye with the rendered torso's measured
+upper boundary, as well as its connected neck, at multiple body scales and
+physical headset heights. Camera checks cover interpolation, heading, tracking
+modes and stale outfit measurements. With drift logging enabled,
+`landing-alignment` reports both neck and visible-collar clearance once per
+tracked pose during impact. Headset confirmation remains necessary.
 
